{ A pawn move changes the squares it attacks forever. Before advancing, inspect its new support and the squares or pieces that depended on its old location. } 1. d5 a6 { Illustrative line. After d5, White gains space and fixes the central pawns. The black knight cannot win the d5-pawn for free: ...Nxd5 would allow exd5, exchanging a knight for a pawn. The illustrative ...a6 leaves the supported center intact. This is a structural example, not a claim that d5 is best in every similar position. White has also closed lines and given up the option of keeping the central tension with d4 against e5. } *

