{ A pawn break challenges an enemy pawn structure. Attacking the support of an advanced pawn can be more practical than attacking that advanced pawn directly. } 3... c5 4. c3 Nc6 { Illustrative line. ...c5 challenges d4. White often reinforces with c3, and ...Nc6 adds another attacker to d4. Both sides have a clear job: Black increases pressure; White maintains the support of e5. The break is not a promise to win a pawn immediately. White can defend, exchange, or change the center. Black should calculate what remains after each exchange and coordinate pieces with that structure. } *

[Event "Think Before Moving a Pawn / 05.2B"]
